The parameters $\hat{\alpha}$ and $\hat{\beta}$ are related to the mean ($\mu_{\lambda}$) and variance ($v_{\lambda}$) of the effective death rate ($\lambda$) as follows:
$$
\begin{aligned}
& \hat{\alpha}=\frac{\mu_{\lambda}}{v_{\lambda}} \\
& \hat{\beta}=\frac{\mu_{\lambda}^{2}}{v_{\lambda}}
\end{aligned}
$$
The parameter $\hat{\alpha}$ is the inverse of the relative variance of $\lambda$ ($\mathrm{RV}_{\lambda}=\frac{v_{\lambda}}{\mu_{\lambda}}$), meaning that $\hat{\alpha}$ decreases as the microbe mortality becomes more heterogeneous between hosts [1]. Similarly, $\hat{\beta}$ (and approximately $\beta$) decreases with increasing heterogeneity in $\lambda$ because $\hat{\beta}$ is the square of the inverse of the coefficient of variation of $\lambda$ ($\mathrm{CV}_{\lambda}=\frac{v_{\lambda}^{1 / 2}}{\mu_{\lambda}}$) [1].
